 @lc9245  Nah, 12gb will be fine for ages. You know when gpus had 8mb, 16mb or 32mb then developers were scaling textures etc such that the exponential increase made sense. The guy with 8mb card used low, 16mb medium and 32mb high. But that's not happening now. If a game uses close to 9gb so it's limiting an 8gb card owner, they aren't using 16gb on high settings or going to make their next game use 16gb or 32gb. Especially not for a card like this which only really makes sense if you use 1080p or 1440p. Indeed looking at the performance figures these cards really make no sense on ultra settings. 60fps isn't smooth, and they often drop below. You're much better off lowering the settings and having closer to monitor refresh fps. You won't even notice the difference in visual quality in the flow of gameplay but you'll certainly feel the difference in smoothness. Remember the people that do these benchmarks and reviews don't play games, they just run benchmarks. They show a clip with a game running at 53 fps and 1% lows of 32, well that's completely unplayable. There are plenty of us still using 1070 with 8gb, that's around 7 years before the amount of vram became a limiting thing, and performance in general is really the bigger issue with this card. You know a 1070 with 12gb ram would still suck for a modern game. But still works because so many people play older games now. 

Similarly the rastering and raytrace performance of the 4070 will be an issue before 12gb vram is too low. i.e it makes no sense at all having a new 8gb card today but it'd be a mistake to think that you need to double it. Which is why the 16gb 4060 ti is so dumb - firstly because it's made people question whether 12gb is enough. If your higher tier cards costing £600 and £800 have 12gb ram then only a twat would release a card at a lower tier with 16gb ram - because the first thing everyone then thinks is "fuck 12gb isn't enough" - and true or not, that's going to hurt the sales of your higher end cards - but, at the same time every reviewer is saying "Don't buy the midrange 4060ti sucks" - so this 4060ti 16gb sku is just moronic. At best there should have been 1 of them with 12gb - and that would have said "12gb works"

The 4060ti 16gb will be junk long before you'd fill the vram you overpaid for. It's almost junk out of the box tbh. It's like if you get a 1tb SSD and you find yourself struggling for space. Get 2tb and most of it will be empty because the extra 1tb is a huge amount of extra space. When disk drives were 1gb then getting 2gb wasn't that much bigger. Simple thing : exponential growth year on year, generation on generation doesn't keep happening. If it did our cpus would have to be 50ghz and we'd have terabytes of system ram by now.

