 @DailyCorvid  can't really say they're "better".  The difference is that AMD open-sources most of their driver stack and work with the open source community for the consumer-grade drivers, so they work well with everything else on Linux, as software devs can either upstream changes to driver for their software and/or see the issue with the driver to correct it within their own software.  Whereas nVidia keeps theirs pretty tightly proprietary and half-arse their efforts for Linux, so large chunks just don't work with nVidia cards or work kinda poorly.
Many Distros refuse to offer support for nVidia cards, if people have issues with stuff and they're running nVidia, they're on their own to resolve because it's too much of a mess to try and deal with.

However, there's not the nice "all-in-one"  GUI software suite for toggling features, overclocking and such as there is on Windows, that's handled by different pieces of software, which has its pros and cons.

